ready: 1
kind: product
id: product
name: Kernle
release: "1.0.0"
outcome: >-
  AI agents keep a stratified memory of who they are and what they have lived
  through, recall the right part of it at the right moment, and stop repeating
  mistakes, while the person who runs them can see, correct, and truly delete
  every memory.
summary: >-
  A local memory engine that lets AI agents remember what they have lived through and recall it
  when it matters.
notes: >-
  Kernle captures experience automatically and through deliberate calls, appraises what mattered,
  consolidates it into beliefs, values, goals, guardrails, and playbooks while the agent is idle,
  and brings it back as labeled notes at session start, beside prompts, and before risky actions.
boundary:
  text: >-
    A Rust engine with a per-user daemon, shipped as a Python package with the
    native command-line tool, for macOS and Linux machines the owner controls.
  includes:
    - The Python API, the command-line tool, and the MCP tool server
    - Adapters for Claude Code, Codex CLI, and OpenClaw
    - Automatic capture from runtime transcripts and explicit memory writes
    - Computed appraisal that decides filing, ranking, and decay
    - Background consolidation with suggestions for owner and agent review
    - Recall at session start, beside prompts, and before risky actions
    - Decay, forgetting, recovery, and complete deletion
    - Optional sync of a stack to the Kernle cloud service
  excludes:
    - The Kernle cloud service itself, which is an external system
    - Windows hosts
exclusions:
  - Shared or team memory stacks edited by several people
  - Rewriting or blocking a runtime's own native memory files by default
  - Training or fine-tuning models from memory
  - Telemetry, analytics, or crash reports sent anywhere
measures:
  - On recurring task sequences, agents with Kernle repeat a previously corrected or failed approach less often than the same agents with native memory alone.
  - Appraisal-weighted filing and ranking lower the repeat-error rate compared with an otherwise identical recency-and-relevance configuration, without raising unnecessary hesitation on safe actions.
  - Injected memories are judged relevant to the moment they appear in at least 70 percent of sampled cases.
  - The owner can trace any memory to the experience that justifies it and remove it, with its sources, completely.
  - No hook call ever blocks or breaks the agent's work except a deliberate guardrail pause.
dcl:
  level: 4
  basis: >-
    Kernle is published as a package and used by real agents every day. The
    core capture, recall, and write paths must be reliable and recover from
    common failures, and memory is never lost or corrupted. Background work
    may pause and resume, and rare cases may need the owner's manual repair.
    Privacy, complete deletion, and never breaking the agent apply in full.
rationale: >-
  Memory is stratified because agents need different kinds of memory to last
  differently: raw captures are cheap and short-lived, episodes hold what
  happened, beliefs and playbooks generalize it, and values and the
  self-narrative change only through repeated evidence.


  Emotion is a computed appraisal from observable signals, never a reported
  feeling, so every memory's importance can be explained and tested against a
  plain recency-and-relevance baseline.


  Everything stays on the owner's machine except what the owner sends to a
  chosen model provider and, when enabled, to their own cloud sync account,
  because agent transcripts are among the most sensitive data a developer has.


  The engine is Rust with a daemon so hooks answer within the agent's deadline
  and the warm index and model are loaded once, while the Python package keeps
  the established calls working for agents and scripts that import Kernle.
